  "summary_en": "Research article presenting the LIAISON project, which proposes an iterative, evidence-based policy process for robots. It explores using compliance tools like the COVR Toolkit as data generators to inform policy, and reports developer findings on standards such as ISO 13482 for personal care, rehabilitation and agricultural robots.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "The policy cycle lags behind robotics innovation, creating legal uncertainty for developers.",
   "Compliance tools can generate data to inform robot policy in a hybrid top-down/bottom-up model.",
   "Developers report inconsistencies and gaps in ISO 13482, especially for vulnerable users such as children, elderly and pregnant women.",
   "New robot applications such as feeding robots often fit poorly into existing legal and standards categories.",
   "Advocates evidence-based, iterative regulation informed by knowledge generated during robot development."
